# Strategy Guru - AI Coding Rules

## Project Overview
Strategy Guru is a comprehensive Chief Product Officer and Strategy Platform built with Next.js 16, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS 4. The platform provides 33 functional pages across 8 main sections for strategic planning, product analytics, customer insights, and business metrics.

## Core Principles

### 1. TypeScript First
- Always use TypeScript with strict mode enabled
- Define proper interfaces for all data structures
- Never use `any` type unless absolutely necessary (prefer `unknown`)
- Use type guards for runtime type checking

### 2. Component Architecture
- Use React Server Components by default
- Add `"use client"` directive only when necessary (state, effects, browser APIs)
- Extract reusable components to `/components/ui/`
- Follow the component pattern: Card, Dialog, Button, Input, etc.

### 3. State Management
- Use Zustand for global state (`store/useStore.ts`)
- Use React `useState` for local component state
- Keep state minimal and derived values computed
- Never mutate state directly

### 4. Styling Standards
- Use Tailwind CSS 4 with `@import "tailwindcss"` syntax
- Follow mobile-first responsive design
- Use CSS variables for theming (see `globals.css`)
- Prefer utility classes over custom CSS
- Use `cn()` utility for conditional class merging

### 5. File Organization
```
app/                    # Next.js App Router pages
  [section]/           # Section folders (strategy, customers, etc.)
    [page]/           # Individual page folders
      page.tsx        # Page component
components/            # Shared React components
  ui/                 # UI component library
  ai-assistant.tsx    # AI Assistant component
lib/                  # Utility functions
store/                # Zustand state management
types/                # TypeScript type definitions
```

### 6. Naming Conventions
- **Files**: Use kebab-case for files (`customer-feedback.tsx`)
- **Components**: Use PascalCase (`CustomerFeedbackPage`)
- **Functions**: Use camelCase (`handleCreateOKR`)
- **Constants**: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (`API_ENDPOINT`)
- **Types/Interfaces**: Use PascalCase (`Objective`, `KeyResult`)

### 7. Data Fetching (Future)
- Prepare for backend integration (currently using mock data)
- Use async/await syntax
- Handle loading and error states
- Show user feedback (alerts, toasts)

### 8. Form Handling
- Always validate required fields
- Disable submit buttons when form is invalid
- Reset form state after successful submission
- Use Dialog component for modal forms
- Show clear user feedback after actions

### 9. Error Handling
- Always handle errors gracefully
- Show user-friendly error messages
- Log errors for debugging (`console.error`)
- Never expose technical details to users

### 10. Performance
- Lazy load heavy components when possible
- Optimize images with Next.js Image component
- Minimize bundle size
- Use React.memo() for expensive renders

## Code Style

### Import Order
```typescript
// 1. React and Next.js imports
import { useState } from "react";
import { useRouter } from "next/navigation";

// 2. Third-party libraries
import { create } from "zustand";

// 3. Internal components
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button";
import { Card } from "@/components/ui/card";

// 4. Types
import type { Objective, KeyResult } from "@/types";

// 5. Utils and constants
import { cn, formatDate } from "@/lib/utils";
```

### Component Structure
```typescript
"use client"; // Only if needed

import { ... } from "...";

// Types defined outside component
interface PageProps {
  params: { id: string };
}

export default function PageName({ params }: PageProps) {
  // 1. State hooks
  const [state, setState] = useState();

  // 2. Store hooks
  const { data } = useStore();

  // 3. Effects
  useEffect(() => {}, []);

  // 4. Handlers
  const handleAction = () => {};

  // 5. Computed values
  const filtered = data.filter(...);

  // 6. Render helpers
  const renderItem = (item) => <div>{item}</div>;

  // 7. JSX return
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

### Dialog/Form Pattern
```typescript
const [showDialog, setShowDialog] = useState(false);
const [formData, setFormData] = useState({ field: "" });

const handleSubmit = () => {
  console.log("Submitting:", formData);
  alert("Success message!");
  setShowDialog(false);
  setFormData({ field: "" }); // Reset form
};

return (
  <>
    <Button onClick={() => setShowDialog(true)}>Open</Button>

    <Dialog open={showDialog} onOpenChange={setShowDialog}>
      <DialogContent>
        <DialogHeader>
          <DialogTitle>Title</DialogTitle>
          <DialogDescription>Description</DialogDescription>
        </DialogHeader>

        <div className="space-y-4">
          <Input
            value={formData.field}
            onChange={(e) => setFormData({...formData, field: e.target.value})}
          />
        </div>

        <DialogFooter>
          <Button variant="outline" onClick={() => setShowDialog(false)}>
            Cancel
          </Button>
          <Button onClick={handleSubmit} disabled={!formData.field}>
            Submit
          </Button>
        </DialogFooter>
      </DialogContent>
    </Dialog>
  </>
);
```

### AI Assistant Integration
```typescript
// Always add at the end of the page, before closing div
<AIAssistant context="okr" /> {/* or analytics, feedback, reports, etc. */}
```

## Data Patterns

### Mock Data Structure
All mock data is in `store/useStore.ts`. When adding new data:
```typescript
interface NewEntity {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  createdAt: Date;
  // ... other fields
}

// In store
const mockNewEntities: NewEntity[] = [
  { id: "1", name: "Example", createdAt: new Date() },
];
```

### Type Definitions
All types are in `types/index.ts`:
```typescript
export interface NewType {
  id: string;
  requiredField: string;
  optionalField?: string;
  arrayField: string[];
  dateField: Date;
  enumField: "option1" | "option2";
}
```

## UI Component Usage

### Card
```typescript
<Card>
  <CardHeader>
    <CardTitle>Title</CardTitle>
    <CardDescription>Description</CardDescription>
  </CardHeader>
  <CardContent>
    Content here
  </CardContent>
</Card>
```

### Button Variants
- `default` - Primary blue button
- `destructive` - Red delete/danger button
- `outline` - Outlined button
- `secondary` - Gray button
- `ghost` - Transparent button
- `link` - Link styled button

### Badge Variants
- `default` - Blue badge
- `secondary` - Gray badge
- `destructive` - Red badge
- `success` - Green badge
- `warning` - Yellow badge
- `outline` - Outlined badge

## Charts (Recharts)

### Common Pattern
```typescript
<ResponsiveContainer width="100%" height={350}>
  <LineChart data={data}>
    <CartesianGrid strokeDasharray="3 3" />
    <XAxis dataKey="name" />
    <YAxis />
    <Tooltip />
    <Legend />
    <Line type="monotone" dataKey="value" stroke="#3b82f6" />
  </LineChart>
</ResponsiveContainer>
```

### Chart Types Used
- `LineChart` - Trends over time
- `BarChart` - Comparisons
- `AreaChart` - Cumulative data
- `PieChart` - Distributions
- `RadarChart` - Multi-dimensional comparisons

## Accessibility

- Always use semantic HTML
- Add `aria-label` for icon-only buttons
- Ensure keyboard navigation works
- Use proper heading hierarchy (h1, h2, h3)
- Provide alt text for images

## Security

- Never commit API keys or secrets
- Sanitize user input
- Validate data on both client and server (future)
- Use environment variables for configuration

## Testing (Future)

- Write tests for utility functions
- Test component rendering
- Test user interactions
- Mock external dependencies

## Git Commit Messages

Format: `<type>: <description>`

Types:
- `feat:` New feature
- `fix:` Bug fix
- `docs:` Documentation changes
- `style:` Code style changes
- `refactor:` Code refactoring
- `test:` Test changes
- `chore:` Build/config changes

Example:
```
feat: Add OKR creation dialog with form validation

- Created Dialog component for modal forms
- Added form state management
- Implemented validation logic
- Reset form after successful submission
```

## When Adding New Features

1. **Define Types First** - Add interfaces to `types/index.ts`
2. **Create UI Components** - Extract reusable parts to `/components/ui/`
3. **Add Mock Data** - Update `store/useStore.ts` with sample data
4. **Build Page** - Create page in appropriate `app/[section]/[page]/page.tsx`
5. **Add Functionality** - Implement handlers, dialogs, forms
6. **Integrate AI** - Add `<AIAssistant context="..." />` if applicable
7. **Test Thoroughly** - Check all interactions, responsive design
8. **Commit** - Use proper commit message format

## Common Utilities

### Date Formatting
```typescript
import { formatDate } from "@/lib/utils";
formatDate(new Date()); // "Jan 24, 2024"
```

### Currency Formatting
```typescript
import { formatCurrency } from "@/lib/utils";
formatCurrency(1234.56); // "$1,234.56"
```

### Class Merging
```typescript
import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";
cn("base-class", condition && "conditional-class"); // Merges classes
```

## AI Assistant Contexts

Available contexts for AI Assistant:
- `okr` - OKR creation and analysis
- `analytics` - Performance insights
- `feedback` - Customer feedback analysis
- `reports` - Report generation
- `competitive` - Competitive intelligence
- `roadmap` - Roadmap planning
- `general` - General assistance

## Future Backend Integration

When adding backend:
1. Keep all API calls in separate service files (`/lib/api/`)
2. Use environment variables for API URLs
3. Add proper error handling
4. Show loading states
5. Update types to match backend responses
6. Remove mock data gradually
7. Add data persistence
8. Implement real AI integration with OpenAI

## Questions to Ask Yourself

Before writing code:
- Is this component reusable? → Extract to `/components/ui/`
- Does this need client interactivity? → Add `"use client"`
- Is there similar code elsewhere? → Create a shared utility
- Will this work on mobile? → Test responsive design
- Is this type-safe? → Add proper TypeScript types
- Can users understand errors? → Add clear error messages
- Is this accessible? → Check keyboard navigation and screen readers

## Remember

- **Mobile-first**: Design for mobile, enhance for desktop
- **Type-safe**: TypeScript catches errors early
- **User feedback**: Always show confirmation/error messages
- **Consistency**: Follow existing patterns in the codebase
- **Documentation**: Update docs when adding major features
- **Performance**: Optimize for speed and bundle size
- **Accessibility**: Make it usable for everyone
- **Future-ready**: Write code that's easy to extend and maintain

## Getting Help

- Check existing components in `/components/ui/` for patterns
- Review similar pages for implementation examples
- Refer to Next.js 16 App Router documentation
- Consult Tailwind CSS 4 documentation
- Review Recharts documentation for chart examples
- Check Zustand documentation for state management
